# Proof

**Claim:** The statement is **false**. The equality does not hold for any $0 < T \leq 1$ (for finite $\alpha$).

## Setup

We have an uncountable family $\{X_t : t \in (0, T]\}$ of independent $N(0,1)$ random variables. Let $Y = \sup_{t \in (0,T]} X_t$.

## Key Observation: The supremum is almost surely $+\infty$

Take any countable subset $\{t_1, t_2, t_3, \ldots\} \subset (0, T]$ (for instance, a countable dense subset). Since $Y \geq \sup_{n} X_{t_n}$, it suffices to show $\sup_n X_{t_n} = +\infty$ a.s.

For any finite $M$, using independence:
$$P\!\left(\sup_{n} X_{t_n} \leq M\right) = P\!\left(\bigcap_{n=1}^{\infty} \{X_{t_n} \leq M\}\right) = \prod_{n=1}^{\infty} P(X_{t_n} \leq M) = \prod_{n=1}^{\infty} \Phi(M).$$

Since $X_{t_n} \sim N(0,1)$ has support on all of $\mathbb{R}$, we have $\Phi(M) < 1$ for every finite $M$. Therefore:
$$\prod_{n=1}^{\infty} \Phi(M) = \lim_{N\to\infty} \Phi(M)^N = 0.$$

So $P(\sup_n X_{t_n} \leq M) = 0$ for every finite $M$. This means:
$$P\!\left(\sup_n X_{t_n} > M\right) = 1 \quad \text{for every finite } M.$$

Taking $M = k$ for $k = 1, 2, 3, \ldots$ and intersecting these probability-1 events:
$$P\!\left(\sup_n X_{t_n} > k \text{ for all } k\right) = 1,$$
which gives $P(\sup_n X_{t_n} = +\infty) = 1$.

Since $Y = \sup_{t \in (0,T]} X_t \geq \sup_n X_{t_n}$, we conclude:
$$P(Y = +\infty) = 1.$$

## Comparing both sides

**Left-hand side:** For any finite $\alpha$,
$$P\!\left(\sup_{t \leq T} X_t \leq \alpha\right) = P(Y \leq \alpha) = 0,$$
since $Y = +\infty$ almost surely.

**Right-hand side:** For any finite $\alpha$ and $0 < T \leq 1$,
$$P(X_1 \leq \alpha)^T = \Phi(\alpha)^T.$$

Since $X_1 \sim N(0,1)$ has full support on $\mathbb{R}$, we have $0 < \Phi(\alpha) < 1$ for every finite $\alpha$. Raising to the power $T > 0$ preserves this: $0 < \Phi(\alpha)^T < 1$.

## Conclusion

For every finite $\alpha$ and every $0 < T \leq 1$:
$$\underbrace{P\!\left(\sup_{t \leq T} X_t \leq \alpha\right)}_{=\, 0} \;\neq\; \underbrace{P(X_1 \leq \alpha)^T}_{>\, 0}.$$

The equality **fails**. The intuition behind the formula $P(X_1 \leq \alpha)^T$ — which correctly describes the maximum of $n$ i.i.d. variables as $P(X_1 \leq \alpha)^n$ — breaks down when passing from a discrete (countable) collection to a continuous (uncountable) collection of independent random variables. With uncountably many independent unbounded random variables, the supremum is almost surely infinite, making the left-hand side identically zero for all finite thresholds.

The statement is **false**.

